     3	The term's second month braided itself the way the tournament autumn had braided, five tracks at once, and Cael ran the braid by schedule and gallery and the reports of people whose eyes he trusted in their own domains — with one difference from every braid before it, which he entered on the month's first night and would reread on its last: *this time every track but mine is a thing the system gave them honestly. Chart what that costs. Chart it before anyone has to pay.*
     4	
     5	---
     6	
     7	Lira's Silver program ran its first internal trial on the second floor at the month's opening — not a meet; a program bout, sanctioned, rated, Rooke's clerk at the table with the continental drill-book, the Silver Rank 5 from the river academy at the rating table as assessor, and across the flags the program's senior third-year, an Iron Rank 6 Force Path who was the best conventional product Rooke's cohort had built before Ephram — and the campus came to watch, because the Silver program's first rated bout under the institution's own seal was a thing the bluff had never staged.
     8	
     9	The floor was the rebuilt second floor, the painted drill-lines still on it, forty feet under the high windows with the striking frames racked along the east wall and the rating table at the north head — a floor built for the drills she had been running for two months, which meant she knew its every line and the third-year knew its every line too, because he had been running them beside her. Cael took the rail with Brom and Karis and ran the instrument.
    10	
    11	**First exchange:** the third-year fought her as the cohort fought — construction, pressure built in courses, the Force doctrine the river academy's Rank 7 had run against her at the confluence, at cohort speed — and the bout's whole premise was whether two months of continental sequences had changed what she did with it. She lost the exchange on flags, cleanly, the way the Zerin file had taught her to lose first exchanges: buying facts. The facts she bought were charted from the rail as she bought them — *he anchors left; his second course sets on a four-count; the structure is real and it has a floor* — and at the break she stood at her mark with the drill-lines under her feet and did the thing that made her Lira, which was not tighten.
    12	
    13	**Second exchange:** she did not run the corner-game. That was the datum, charted from the rail before the exchange was a minute old — the taxation economy that had beaten the confluence and taken an exchange off Zerin, left in the kit. She ran, instead, the program: the sequenced-burst intervals the continental drill-book prescribed, cold at the confluence, right at the wool town, and now, on a painted floor, at cost — and she took the whole exchange forward of the drill-lines' second mark, closing at a stride and a half where the cohort was built to fight at three, so that the third-year's second course never set, because she was through its four-count on the third and already inside the distance the course needed. Flags hers. The gallery's sound was the sound of a story changing shape, and Rooke at the floor's head wrote one word.
    14	
    15	**Third exchange:** the third-year adapted — the cohort adapted; that was its whole doctrine — and built faster, and the exchange was the bout's honest contest: the built grammar against a cohort at its own speed, and the grammar held. She ran Zerin's intervals as a *language* now, not a drill, and the language had tenses the third-year's construction could not price in time, and the flags came to her one and then two — both of them taken on her back foot at the floor's east third, with the racked frames a pace behind her heel and no ground left to give, because the grammar did not need ground — and Cael's chart recorded the datum the whole program existed to produce: *she can run the built version's grammar at Iron tempo against the cohort's best. Two months. The Rank 5 at the rating table has stopped writing.*
    16	
    17	**Fourth exchange:** she closed it, on points, at range, the way the confluence had taught her to close a bout against a builder — no felling blow, the structure declined until it stood on empty floor. The flags went up. The trial went to the program's entrant, three exchanges to one, and Lira walked to the flags breathing hard and shook the third-year's hand, and the Rank 5 came down from the rating table and said something short that the delegation extracted at dinner: "He said, 'Two months. Zerin took two years to run that sequence at Iron rate. Rooke's file is right.'"
    18	
    19	The bill came at the rail ten minutes later, because Rooke collected what he invoiced and had never once let a fighter walk off a floor without the price entered. He ran her through it standing, in the coaching file's own order: the burst-shoulder, tight — she rolled it twice and he watched the second roll and wrote; the landing-leg, honest; the fourth exchange's last two closes taken at range because the intervals had cost her the reach to take them any other way. "Sequenced bursts at Iron tempo, nine of them," he said. "Nine is two more than the drill-book budgets in a rated bout. You spent Silver-program coin to beat a cohort third-year and you'll pay for it Thirdsday." The ruling went in the file: two days at half work, the shoulder iced, no frames until the shoulder reported clean. "Cheap," Rooke said. "It's cheap now. It stops being cheap on a road with three bouts in four days. Learn the difference before the meet and not at it."
    20	
    21	She paid it properly, and the file noted that too, and Cael entered the nine in his own chart beside the drill-book's seven, because a number a coach called expensive was a number worth carrying.
    22	
    23	The third-year came to the rail on his way out with his kit over his shoulder and the flat courtesy of a man who had been beaten by something he intended to understand. "The four-count," he said. "How long have you had it?"
    24	
    25	"Since the second week. I couldn't run it at speed until the sixth."
    26	
    27	"So you've had it four weeks and you didn't use it on me until the second exchange." He shifted the kit. "Why?"
    28	
    29	"Because I wanted to know whether your second course sets on the four-count every time or only when you're ahead." Lira said it without any weight at all. "It's every time."
    30	
    31	The third-year took that away with him, which was the correct thing to do with it, and Cael charted the exchange as the evening's second finding: *she bought the first exchange on purpose in front of a rating table, which costs a flag on the record, and she did it anyway, and Rooke wrote one word.*
    32	
    33	Rooke's review came at the flags, two words, Rooke-length, delivered to the coaching file and the fighter in the same breath: "Two months." He wrote nothing else. The Rank 5 said, "Two months," back, as a datum, and the two coaches looked at each other with the specific satisfaction of men whose instrument had just returned a number they could stand on — and neither of them said the word the file had carried since the confluence, because the file's word was for the road, and the road was six weeks out.
    34	
    35	She came to the rail afterward with her collar dark and her hands taped and the look Cael had catalogued for two months, and this time — because the program had a bout in it now, and a flag, and a Silver's word — she gave it to him in words.
    36	
    37	"Three years ago I'd have told you I didn't need any of this." She nodded at the painted lines, the frames, the clerk with the drill-book, the whole apparatus built toward her. "Scrapyard pride. I was very good at it. It got me a ledger and a circuit and a crown and a station with a door." She rolled the taped hands, watching them. "I'm better now. I can admit I want it."
    38	
    39	Karis, at his other shoulder, said nothing, and wrote nothing, and afterward, on the walk back, said only: "She used the word *admit*. I've been waiting three years for that verb. Enter it under her name in the notebook's permanent section, not the working one."
    40	
    41	"Noted," Cael said, and wrote it down this time, in the observation notebook, at the rail, where she could see him do it, and she watched the pen and did not stop it, which was the whole transaction.
    42	
    43	*She wants it,* the log got that night. *She said so, on a floor, after a Silver stopped writing. In three years I have never once heard her say she wanted a thing the system could give her — she called them wrong, or late, or owed. Wanted is new. File the date beside the corridor's. Whatever this term costs, it now has a price on it that isn't mine, and the price has her name.*
    44	
    45	---
    46	
    47	Brom's paperwork arrived in two envelopes in the same week, and he opened them in different rooms.
    48	
    49	The first was the district seat's: the advancement review's formal docket confirmation, panel constituted, the last Fifth-day of term, the season record entered as evidence, a line at the foot Bracken read aloud at the counter with the satisfaction of a registrar adding one to a tally he had kept for thirty years — *outcome anticipated: advancement, per title.* "They've written *anticipated* on a docket," Bracken said. "The registry does not write *anticipated* on dockets. Somebody at the district seat has decided the ink is cheaper than the pretense." He handed it over. "Iron-tier, by the last Fifth-day. The plaque's ribbon can go on your collar now. Nobody will think it's presumption."
    50	
    51	Brom read it once and folded it and put it in the kit, and the ribbon stayed in the kit, and the table noted where the ribbon stayed.
    52	
    53	The second envelope was Velmere-sealed.
    54	
    55	It came up with the evening post — the estate's mark in the wax, the hand the delegation had seen twice a year for two years and once, at Norhold, on a single line — and he did not take it to the courtyard. He took it upstairs, and it lay on the small table in his room — Lira reported this, because Lira reported things — for two days, unopened, beside the docket, the two envelopes squared together with the care he gave to useful things and neither of them touched. Lira reported it the way Lira reported things, which was as logistics, at the residence table, to nobody in particular, with the drill-book open in front of her and her eyes on the page. "Second envelope's still on his table. Squared with the docket. Two days." She turned a leaf she was not reading. "He moved the lamp so it isn't in the light."
    56	
    57	"You went into his room," said Karis.
    58	
    59	"I went past his door. Twice a day, because the stair goes past his door." She turned another leaf. "I'd like it noted that I didn't go in, which for me is growth, and that I wanted to, which for me is honesty."
    60	

