   121	"Name for the door log?"
   122	
   123	"There isn't one on the instrument," the counsel said, before Cael could answer. "The instrument says *the enrollee of record, unclassified.* Enter what the instrument says."
   124	
   125	The officer entered what the instrument said. It took eleven seconds. Cael watched the pen do it and filed them where the other eleven seconds lived, and went in through a door that had been opened for the term because the ordinary one was not built for the people who had come to watch.
   126	
   127	The registry seat's proceedings chamber was the exchange's old selling floor, cleared: a long room with the lot positions still cut into the boards, the registry's sigil on the end wall, the presiding seat on a low platform beneath it, the evaluation seat west of the seal, the records table behind the platform and below it, the two parties' tables facing — petitioner to the northwest, respondent to the northeast — and a public gallery of forty benches that the correspondents filled to the rails. Cael counted the room the way he counted every room, and the count came back with the same absence in it three times.
   128	
   129	The presiding seat was occupied. A registrar in the registry's senior grey with the seat's hood forward, so that there was no face in it to read at all — and Cael pointed the whole instrument at the posture and the hands for an hour and got a reading he entered that night with the flat care he kept for failures: *the hands rest where the code puts them and move when the procedure moves them: the minute, the schedule, the receipt. No adjusting, no waiting, no weight shifted on the bench in forty minutes. Four years of reading men who were managing their faces, and it turns out the face was a courtesy. This one isn't managing anything, because there is nothing being managed. A posture, doing a seat's work.* The record identified the seat by seal. The clerk read the seal's designation into the minute at the session's opening and the registrar did not add a name, because the code did not require one, and the whole long room proceeded to conduct the most consequential proceedings of the enrollee's life in front of a chair whose occupant was, by procedure, nobody.
   130	
   131	The gallery held the correspondents at the rail and, behind them, the ordinary curious — Ostrand's registry-literate, clerks and factors and two guild officers, come to watch a chair with no name conduct the continent's most-read case — and among them, in a plain coat, at the gallery's end where the sightlines to every table and door converged, the delegation's assessment clerk, who had come down on the coach as records support and had taken, without appearing to choose, the seat a man takes when he intends to read a room and not be read by it. Cael marked him from the respondent's table and did not look again. Withrow sat at the table's head in her registry grey with the response in its two cases beside her, and did not touch them, because a chancellor at a proceeding did not fidget with her own filing. The counsel sat with the minute-book open. Karis sat with the notebook closed, which was its own announcement.
   132	
   133	Jent entered the petition. It took nine minutes. He read the instrument's operative paragraphs into the record in the voice of a man reading a schedule, tendered the exhibit list — Umber's referred tournament file among the exhibits, the *unscorable under standard* entry now evidence — and sat down at the correct height. The counsel entered the response, which took twenty-two minutes because two hundred and six items took twenty-two minutes to summarize even at a registry pace, and the seal received both without expression, and confirmed the schedule, and adjourned.
   134	
   135	The evaluation seat was empty; its empanelment would be confirmed at a later session, the notice said, when the district's senior evaluators had been canvassed. The records table was staffed — a senior records officer and, behind him, on rotation, a junior with a private carefulness Cael knew from three postings and two academies. Havel did not look at the respondent's table once in forty minutes. From Havel, four years and five note-entries deep, the not-looking had the weight of a sentence.
   136	
   137	Withrow, at the session's close, did the one thing the code permitted a respondent chancellor and nothing more: she rose when the seal rose, and inclined her head to the seat at the depth the registry taught its officers to incline to a seat, and Cael, watching, charted the gesture's exactness — twenty years of registry courtesy, performed at a chair whose occupant she would never be able to name — and understood that she was addressing the seal on purpose, as a seal, to enter into her own ledger that she had given the institution its due and would expect the same from it. She said nothing on the stairs. In the coach she said: "I have bowed to that seat three hundred times from the other side of it. Today I learned what it looks like from this one. Note that I bowed anyway."
   138	
   139	The coach's council on the river road ran the session the way the delegation ran everything, in order of arrival. The counsel first: "Procedurally immaculate. He entered nothing he didn't need. I entered everything we had. The seat received both as if they weighed the same." Karis: "They do weigh the same. To the seat." Brom, after the length of time Brom took: "Havel didn't look at you." Cael: "No." Brom: "He looked at you at Greyvane. Twice, from the delegation gallery. I counted." Lira, to the window: "So the junior's stopped learning too." And Seln, from the corner, eyes closed, delivering the session's only finding in the register the table weighed at full freight: "The records officer at the senior chair is district grade. The junior behind him is monitoring grade, on rotation. The seat is registry-seat grade — the seal's grade, not a person's. Three grades of officer in one room and not one of them is the grade that decides anything. The office notes it." He did not open his eyes. "The office has sat in rooms like that. From the other chair."
   140	
   141	Cael filed the session's observation on the coach back up the river road, and it was the observation the whole term would keep returning to, and the log held it whole:
   142	
   143	*The hearing at Greyvane had Coss's face on it — you could read the whole case off him from across the room. The inspection had Vastin's; the evaluation was the man. This has a seal. A registrar of no age and no face reading a designation off a clerk's card, and a procurator with nothing on him, and a room conducting itself. I keep looking for the person and finding the hand.*
   144	
   145	---
   146	
   147	He wrote the month's close at the window, with the docket dates on the sill — Lira's meet, Brom's review, the second session, the blank — and the entry came the way honest entries came, not planned, just true enough to write down:
   148	
   149	*Everyone I came here with is holding something the system finally gave them honestly. Lira's ladder. Brom's number. Karis's shelf. Write it down now, while it's simple: whatever this challenge costs me, it should not cost them.*
   150	
   151	Karis read the entry over his shoulder — she had come to the window with a draft, and had stopped, and had read, which she did not do, and he let her, which he did not do — and said, in her flattest register: "That is not how any of us are built."
   152	
   153	He wrote that down too, underneath, in the log's own parenthesis:
   154	
   155	*(Karis read this entry over my shoulder and said "that is not how any of us are built." Noted. Filed. Afraid of it.)*
   156	
   157	Then he read the parenthesis back to her aloud, because she had stood at his shoulder long enough to have earned the hearing.
   158	
   159	She listened to it with her arms folded and the draft still in her hand, and did not soften a word of it.
   160	
   161	"Afraid of it," she said. "Good. Be afraid of it accurately — with dates, and a column, and a total at the foot — and on the day it starts costing them you will know it was that day and not some other. That is what a notebook is for."

