editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:133:The presiding seat was occupied. A registrar sat in it, in the senior grey of the registry, the hood of the seat drawn forward over the head, so that from the house's table there was nothing to see under it but shadow. The hands lay on the bench in front of the hood. They were an ordinary pair of hands, a little lined, neither young nor old, a man's or a woman's, Cael could not have sworn which. The clerk at the side of the platform stood and read into the minute the seat's designation: the seat of the registry at Ostrand, sitting by its seal in proceedings the registry had designated institutional. He read no name. The hood offered none.
