editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:127:He thought about the quiet thing he carried, sealed, and the debt it was sealed for, which the man at the copying table did not know he was owed. He thought that it would be a very hard debt to pay to somebody who would not let anything be paid to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:163:They weighed it in the post room behind the counter, on the brass beam the house used for parcels going down the hill, where letters were weighed for their postage and nobody had ever weighed anything that mattered. The answer had been bound in two parts, because the binder's thread would not go through it in one, and the two parts lay side by side in a leather document wallet with the house's seal on the flap. Bracken set the wallet on the pan himself. He put on the brass weights one at a time from the box, the big ones first, and then the small, and then the very small ones that lived in their own velvet slots, until the beam came level and stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:181:"Three, at a walk." Withrow came in and laid one hand on the wallet's flap, over the house's seal. "Then three go down it. The head groom and his son on the greys. Your senior clerk in the middle, with this across his saddle where the town can see it. Fifth-day, the second bell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:221:He started at the end, with the morning after, because that was where a person actually lived. The ferryman with a letter, the guild hall with a letter, the clerk with a sealed file and no key. A daughter at Withrow's counter every week for a year, asking whether anybody had begun the review, and nobody ever obliged to begin it. Then the three steps of chapter fourteen in Bracken's voice that would not let itself be a voice, and the third word that nobody had ever had to stamp. Then the six slips of paper laid out down the long table like stones across a stream, each a door, each real law, each opening toward the next and never back the way you had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:1:# Chapter 10 — Per Seal
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:11:*The presiding seat: per seal.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:19:"Per seal," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:21:"It's in the second volume," said Karis. "Toward the back, in the sections nobody reads unless they're looking for something to be frightened of. Some matters the registry decides belong to the office and not the officer. In those, the seat sits as itself, not as whoever is in it. The code even gives it a hood for the purpose." She did not unfold her arms. "The minute takes the seal's impression and no signature. In law there's nobody under the hood to name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:41:"To the registry," he said. "Which is the seat. Which is the seal." He folded the notice once along its crease. "Go and find the section, and when you've found it come and tell me whether I'm wrong. I should very much like to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:47:*Per seal. Jent, the name off the last leaf. Seventeen days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He read it at his own window that night, with the fountain going in the court and the copy of the instrument shut in its folder on the bed behind him. Across the top of the grid ran the columns: the sitting, the panel, the day, the finding, the seal. Down the side ran the three times his place on the bluff had been written down by somebody obliged to write it. The grant at Greyvane, from the hearing. The first renewal, at Halcenvane, in the inspection year, before the Compact's delegation. The renewal at the mill town, last year, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:149:But the house's copy was not the only one. Bracken had told him so at the counter two days afterward, when the line went into the enrollment book. The delegation's counsel had asked, in writing, that the registry's own copy of the note go up the road under the delegation's countersignature before the registry sealed it into his file, and Bracken had objected on both sides of a sheet, and lost. So that copy had waited. And when it came back down, sealed and dated, so that the enrollee of record could initial its receipt before it went up again for good, Gault had called him into his office to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:155:*Gault's office. Initialled the registry's copy of the renewal, back from the delegation under seal, dated today. Up the road again in the noon bag.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:159:He went back to the table on the sill. Its first renewal's row gave one day, the day of the trestle, which was the day the house's own enrollment book carried in Bracken's small upright hand, because that was the day the house had seen it happen. The registry's file, wherever it sat, would carry the other day, the day its own copy was sealed. The house had never kept a copy of that one. It had gone back up the road the same afternoon. The only place on the continent where both days stood side by side was a book he had filled himself and shut in a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:187:She went on ruling. Every row this time gave two days, the trestle and the seal, and the first renewal's row gave the reason for the gap in a single line. Under the grid she wrote where each day could be found: the house's enrollment book for the trestle; the registry's own file, to be produced on request, for the seals; and for the gap, *the enrollee's contemporaneous record, available to the seat*. Then she reached across the table for the instrument, opened it at schedule two, and laid it beside the old table she had just taken out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:191:He read them together. Schedule two listed his file's standing entries by the registry's own dates, and the registry dated a renewal by its seal. Her old table dated it by the trestle. Two papers on one desk, about one afternoon, nine days apart.

