editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:"Here's what I think, then. Today the work that counts has no instrument pointed at it. Every one of them will be looking at the brass and the frame and the figures and thinking that's where the work is, and nobody in that room will ever know what the corner cost you." He reached down and knocked twice on the top of Cael's head with the knuckles of his good hand, softly. "I'll know. That's the best I've got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:81:The delegation's counsel had the nearest, and she was untying the ribbon of a second folder with her teeth because both her hands were full of the first. Next to her Ilsev was sharpening a pencil with a small knife, carefully, into a fold of paper on her knee, so as to leave no shavings on the wing's floor. Beyond Ilsev, Havel was writing the date at the head of a ruled sheet on a board across his knees, with the bound file under the board. As Cael came in he looked up for about four-tenths of a second, level and without expression, and then down again to finish his date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:101:"Enrollee. You've stood on this oak before and you know my three rules." Gault gave them flatly, as he would read off a dial. "Say so when you cannot. Give me the same thing twice before you give me your best once. And you are measured against the line you drew here yourself in your second month, and against nothing else: not against hopes, not against anything printed, not against talk." He looked up. "Show me a marvel and I shall have to wonder what I missed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:3:The Mire instructor racked the fourth weight with her own hands and tried the pawl twice with her thumb, listening to it, before she cranked the rail to a height nobody named aloud and went back to her chair and buried her hands in her coat pockets again. The clerk drew a fresh square in chalk on the oak beneath the rail and returned to his desk. Nobody told Cael which rack the weight hung from, which was the point.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:71:Meanwhile his right arm held a thing that could have met the weight twice as often and handed half of every blow to the oak. He had taken it from a fighter who had beaten him fairly, and it was written down in a circuit record far from here and not in this one, and the man across the table had just stood up for him over something that was not quite true. Twice now in one term Cael had given him something true with a piece cut out of it, in his own room, for his own book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:151:Everybody had looked at the frame first, which was only natural, and then everybody had looked back at him. Gault: twice, short and level, because he had a line to write and needed to see what he was writing about. The Mire instructor: four times, her eyes going from boy to frame and back like a woman adding up a column. The Ash instructor: twice, over the top of her book. The counsel: three times, and long, the way counsel look at an incident, to pin it before it moves. Havel: twice, the second quicker than the first. Ilsev: to the tray of dust, to the clerk's shoes, to Cael, and to the dust again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:159:You look twice at a thing you did not expect. That isn't manners or temper or training; it's what eyes are for. The first look tells you that something has happened and the second tells you what, and six people in that room had needed the second look. The fourth chair had not. One look had given him the whole of what happened on the oak. He had weighed it on the spot and filed it, and Cael held nothing further that another look could fetch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:285:*He didn't look twice.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:291:*Two lines in his book. I'll be a long time wondering.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:47:Cael read it twice. Then he read the line above it, the baseline from his second month, and the line above that, the enrollment itself, with the folder marked in pencil *Ninety-four minutes.* lying beside the book where Bracken had clearly laid it on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:93:Brom did not often do that. He remembered exact words better than most people remembered faces, but he kept the gift in a drawer and took it out perhaps twice a year, when he thought a sentence had been made with care and ought not to be worn down by handling. He took it out now with his bowl in front of him and his strapped hand flat beside the bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:203:Asked, he would have offered the reason he had given himself at the gate. A look from a man in his coat was a thing the boy would have to carry. He would have to write it down somewhere and decide what it had meant, and then decide again in a month when he knew more. Havel had watched the boy's face at the pump step in Ardenmere, two years ago, and he had watched it at the end of a long hearing table at Greyvane. He had seen what it cost the boy to be looked at by men in that coat. A records officer could not do much about that, but he could decline to add to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:207:The rest was the pear. He had never been able to put the pear into any sentence that made sense, and he did not try now. A boy on a pump step had held out a pear in a twist of paper to the officer questioning him, and the officer had wanted it and had not taken it. Ever since, Havel had felt that he had been offered something once that he had not been entitled to refuse. A man who has refused a thing like that does not go looking up at tiers afterward. He does not get a second offer, and he ought not to look as though he were hoping for one.
43:Havel brought the second round of the dispatch case in at the half after noon, and saw only the paper.
47:That night, in his narrow room at the inn behind the east tower, he wrote a fifth. At its head he put the line from the return, which he had read upside down on her desk in perhaps three seconds and found he had entire.

