editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:13:"You asked me once not to count your bursts," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:19:"Anything. His stamps. His settles. How long he laughs. You'll see things from the rail I won't see from the floor, and I won't be able to ask you about them till it's over, and I want them all, in order, when I do." She finished the wrist and flexed the hand, and looked at it as if it belonged to somebody she was fond of. "Karis will count my bursts whether I like it or not. That's her business. Yours is him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:29:"Program trial. My rule, not the region's." He held up his board as if the rule were written on it, which it was not. "Four exchanges. Each one, the table gives a flag to whoever took it, or to nobody if nobody did. Most flags takes the trial. Three bursts a bout for the program's entrant, as on every day she's ever stood on this floor. Past three, the table still gives the exchange to whoever takes it, and the card pays for every burst over. The other fighter, what his own Path allows. Level after four, the table rates them both, and the higher figure takes it." He sat down. "The figure goes in this program's book. Not the registry's. Not yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:39:From the rail there was nothing else to watch. A Force fighter's shove went on contact, and this one's contact was the floor. He stamped with his front foot, and a beat later the boards ahead of the stamp, out to a stride, jumped, as a table jumps when somebody slaps it from beneath, and went on jumping for a count or two before they lay down. Then the shove came back up into him along the line it had gone out by, and his knees gave and his hips dropped a finger's breadth while he took it. That was the settle. Cael could see it from the rail and hear the boards answer it. It was the bill for the last stamp, paid before the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:47:It was the worst thing she could have done, and the whole gallery knew it. She did not run the four-count. She did not lie. She went at him in a plain hard line, burst one off the left hip, the old burst, the one she had brought out of a barn at fourteen and that every fighter on the hill had seen a hundred times. The burst carried her twenty feet and put her down inside his stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:77:The first step was an ordinary step, any fighter's, and the Force watched it come and stamped again. The second step was the lie. She put her weight on her front foot and left it there one count too long, the long lie the Stone had taught her, so that anybody watching for the burst would see it coming and set himself to meet it. The Force saw it coming. He stamped exactly where her burst would land if it came off that front foot, a good hard stamp, and Cael heard the boards jump there a beat later and thought it was the best the man had done all morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:89:She burst off the back foot. Not the front foot she had lied with, which stood on boards that had jumped a moment ago; the back one, which had never left wood he had touched. She burst at the angle the first three counts had sworn she would never take, low and to the right, past the place he had stamped, and came down on still boards two strides from his left hip with her landing beat holding her steady. The Force was still in his settle, knees bent, taking his own push back, with nothing to put it into.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:97:"Two bursts," said Karis, writing. "Two exchanges. One flag each."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:109:She tried the four-count once, and the lie on the second count put her front foot on boards that had been still a moment earlier and were jumping now, and her foot went sideways, and she went with it. She did not burst. She scrambled, two ugly running steps and a hop, out of the jumping boards on her own legs, and came up near the east wall beside the striking frames on their thick springs. He came after her. Not fast; Force fighters were never fast. Stamp, settle, step, stamp, a stride at a time, the floor ahead of him jumping like water coming up a beach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:125:Burst three. Off the right foot, the still one, straight along the line of the frames, not toward him at all but past him, so close to the frames that the nearest post rang as her shoulder brushed it. She came down eight feet on, behind the end of his fan, on boards he had not stamped because every stamp of his had been in front of him. The Force was turning, and a man turning in his own settle turns slowly. He was a beat late.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:131:"Three bursts," said Karis. "Her whole bout." Her pen kept moving. "One exchange to go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:135:The Force had learned twice now. He was not going to give her a settle to burst into. He was not going to chase her into a corner where she could go past him along a wall. When the clerk said *exchange* he did not come forward at all. He stamped where he stood, to the left, and to the right, and in front, and behind, a stride out on each side, and as each patch of boards lay down he stamped it awake again, turning slowly in the middle, until he stood in a ring of floor that never stopped jumping. It was like a well with the water always moving at its edges. He stood on the still wood in the middle of it and waited for her to come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:139:Rooke's rule was three bursts. She had spent three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:145:Burst four, off the left hip, in one long low flight to the edge of the ring, the old burst, the barn's burst, the strongest thing she had ever owned. She came down on jumping boards. They moved. She let them. She let them slide her landing a hand's breadth and turned the slide into the start of the next thing, as she had learned in the dust on the honest floor, and burst again off the moving boards before they could take her any further.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:147:Burst five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:159:"Three flags to one," said the clerk into the noise, and wrote it in the red book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:163:"Execution, ten. Effect, ten." Rooke did not look up. "Control, seven. Twenty-seven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:167:"Ten is a good ordinary day at the heart of Silver. She was there in two columns." His voice did not change at all. "The seven is the fourth burst and the fifth. Up to three, a fighter owns her bout. Past three she rents it, and the card collects the rent." He looked at the clerk. "Enter it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:169:Twenty-seven. Cael had her figures in his notebook since the wool town, every one: twenty-five, twenty-six, twenty-seven at her very best last year, the top of what the board called strong Iron. Twenty-eight was the floor of the Silver bracket. She had stood a point short of it before. She stood a point short of it now. But this time the point had a name and a place, and Rooke had just said both aloud in front of the whole house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:179:"From the hip to above the knee, down the outside," said Rooke, to his board, and stood up. "That's four and five. Three never goes there." He wrote. "Two days at half work. No frames. No bursts at all until the leg is quiet, and you'll tell me when it's quiet, and I'll decide whether to believe you." He tucked the pencil behind his ear. "At the meet you don't fight one bout in a morning. You fight one, and eat, and fight again. A fifth burst before noon is a leg that won't carry you after it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:93:She was an Iron Rank Five of the third year, with a long patient guard that liked to make you come to it, and a habit of holding her edge-line a hand's breadth lower than the cohort taught, so that a man who read the line by the cohort's rule stepped onto it. Cael read her weight instead of her line, and went to her slowly in the first exchange and took it with a burst off the left foot when her back heel lifted. In the second she walked him into the corner by the south door, one patient step at a time, and he spent a second burst getting out of it and came out a stride short of where he wanted to be. The third was the rotation's: it went to her, whatever it cost, and it cost him her edge-line laid across his forearm, hard, a sting that went through the wrap and stayed there for an hour. In the fourth he waited for the heel again and it came, and the touch was his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:99:On the twenty-seventh evening, at the north wall, the coats were early.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:129:Cael knew the Force a little. A broad-backed young man who laughed when he was surprised; Rank Seven on the porter's wall since the last sitting of the old year, six ranks above Lira's one, and the best fighter the cohort had made in a straight line, without a program, since Ephram. A Force fighter's Path was a shove, and it went on contact. This one put it into the floor through his front foot. He stamped, and a beat later the boards ahead of the stamp, out to a stride, jumped, and went on jumping for a count or two before they settled. Then the shove came back up into him, the way it always came back into a Force fighter along the line it had gone out by, and he had to take it in his knees before he could stamp again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:155:She tried the second count outside the lane this time, short of it, and the third count, the empty one, with one foot in the lane and one out. The lane took the foot that was in. She stood through it. She let it slide her front foot, the way a fighter lets a push go by, and on the fourth count she burst off the back foot, the one that was still on honest boards, across the lane and through the end of it, and came out of the burst three strides from Brom with the dust still running behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:163:She ran it, and it was better: the empty count taken standing, one foot in and one foot out, the lane sliding the inside foot and Lira letting it. She burst on the fourth off the honest foot, and the burst carried her a stride from Brom, and she touched his shoulder. Brom nodded. Lira turned round, grinning, to say something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:189:Nobody spoke for a moment. Then Lira took the pencil stub out of her pocket and wrote on her palm, under the place where the tape stopped. "Ten, ten, twelve," she said. "Clean at eleven, eleven, thirteen." She looked up. "That's the last one tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:195:Karis, on the stair, read her column back aloud without being asked: *First lane, slow through ten. Second, through ten. Third, through twelve; clean at thirteen. Not tested past three.* Then she shut the notebook, said she had what she had come for, and went up to bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:203:"Not to put my weight down on the third count," she said. "I've been doing the lie with a real foot. On his floor the real foot is the one that moves. So I lie with a foot I don't care about, and I keep the one I'm going to burst from on wood that holds still." She turned her head on the boards. "And I go on the fourth. Not the third. Everything I've ever done says go on the third, and against him the third is when the boards are still jumping from the last stamp. The fourth is when he's taking his own push back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:211:It was the first new thing the lane had told him since the morning it settled. He had believed the ten counts were the price, fixed, like the five free bursts on boards; a thing he could plan around, write in a minute before a fight, and pay. Now he knew they were not fixed. They grew when he spent the lane over and over. He did not know yet by how much, or whether they grew without end, or whether they came back down with rest. He knew only that a figure he had thought was a wall was a slope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:223:He thought about it on the stair, and in his room, and at the window with Hesk's book shut on his knee. In the morning he went down to the east hall before the first bell and found Rooke at the trestle with the red book, and told him: three lanes in a night, ten and ten and twelve, and nothing past three. Rooke listened without looking up. Then he wrote one word on the corner of his board, and turned the board so that Cael could not see it, and said, "Good. Now don't do it again until you know why."

